This distinction can be important for applications such as active learning. A typical strategy is to query examples for which H(p(y|x, D)) is large (where H(p) is the entropy, discussed in Section 6.1). However, this could be due to uncertainty about the parameters, i.e., large H(p(θ|D)), or just due to inherent variability of the outcome, corresponding to large entropy of p(y|x, θ). In the latter case, there would not be much use collecting more samples, since our epistemic uncertainty would not be reduced. See [Osb16] for further discussion of this point.

